{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he sent out the raven, etc.</b> One needs to know why he sent it. And if it was to see whether the waters had abated — Scripture should have said ״הֲקַלּוּ הַמַּיִם״ as it says concerning the sending of the dove. And if there was no need to say it, why was it necessary to say it regarding the sending of the dove? Further, what is the intention of his saying <b>going forth and returning until the waters dried up</b>, etc.? If the intention is that it found no rest outside and would return, then why did Noah need to send the dove — and was not the raven itself a sign and proof that the waters had not yet abated? Further, what is his intention in informing ״until the waters dried up״? Further, why did it need to go out until the waters dried up, since once the dove went out it would have been sufficient to know? Further, why did Scripture not say ״וַיִּשְׁלַח יָדוֹ וַיִּקָּחֶהָ״ as is said concerning the dove?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intention of Scripture is according to the statement of our Sages, may their memory be for a blessing (Sanhedrin 108b), that the raven served in the ark, and Noah knew this about it. Therefore, when he opened the window of the ark, he drove the raven out of the ark immediately and at once. And that is what is meant by <b>and he sent out the raven</b>, and he did not mention ״to see whether the waters had abated״ as he said afterward concerning the sending of the dove. And it was the raven that would go out and return — meaning: Noah would drive it away and it would return, and it remained in this manner until the drying of the waters. And for the purpose of a mission to see whether the waters had abated, he sent the dove — and that is what is meant by <b>and he sent out the dove from him</b>, meaning: on a mission for him to see whether the waters had abated. And this thing he could not have learned from the raven, since it was being driven out and was not on a mission — unlike the dove, which went on a mission and gave him an answer that she found no resting place for the sole of her foot, and immediately he sent his hand and took her and brought her into the ark, as it says: <b>and he sent out his hand and took her and brought her to himself, into the ark</b> — meaning: to the place designated for her for her safekeeping. Unlike the raven, which as a dispatched [creature] was left [outside], and when it would return Noah would send it away again." "<b>And further,</b> it may be explained according to their statement, may their memory be for a blessing (Sanhedrin 108b), and this is their language: Resh Lakish said: the raven gave Noah an irrefutable answer. It said to him: Your Master hates me and you hate me. Your Master hates me — ״of every clean animal, etc. seven and seven,״ and you hate me — you left a pair [from my kind] and sent one of a pair. Were I to encounter the angel of heat or the angel of cold, the world would be missing one creature. Or perhaps you need my mate? He said to him: Wicked one! What is permitted to me is forbidden — what is forbidden to me, all the more so! This is the extent of his language. Now, according to this midrash, the wicked one did not want to go on a mission lest the angel of cold, etc., encounter him, or because he suspected Noah concerning his mate. And therefore Noah needed to send the dove. And this is the meaning of the verse: <b>and he sent out the raven</b> — but it did not want to go; rather, it would go out and return. From this Noah knew that the waters were still upon the face of all the earth, but he did not know whether they had abated or not, for there is no proof from the raven since it did not move from its place. Therefore he sent the dove to know this particular detail — whether the waters had abated. And for this reason it was necessary to say <b>הֲקַלּוּ הַמַּיִם</b> in the sending of the dove, to say that for this particular detail it was necessary to send her. And understand." "And the explanation of the words of the statement is along these lines: the raven came with a complaint following the manner of the verse of the prophet (Psalms 69:27): ״אֲשֶׁר הִכִּיתָ רָדְפוּ״ — meaning, for it is fitting to have mercy on one who is stricken and afflicted, yet they added pursuit. And this is the claim of the raven in saying: Your Master hates me, for He diminished the taking of my species, and you added hatred — for if I were to encounter, etc., the world would be found, etc., and behold you are behaving with cruelty. Or, when we say that you did not act out of cruelty, one must judge you as necessarily having another reason, namely that perhaps for my wife, etc. — meaning: even though she is lesser in degree, perhaps because your wife was forbidden to you, you thought that only your wife was forbidden and none other, and you needed my female. And there is no difficulty as to what makes his wife different from all of them, for regarding each one this question applies." "<b>Or,</b> according to their statement, may their memory be for a blessing (Sanhedrin 108b), the raven served in the ark. On this basis it claimed that Noah desired only the raven, for he saw its service, and they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Avodah Zarah 20b): gazing at a thing begets desire, and they forbade the matter. And Noah answered him regarding what he said ״your Master״ and not ״his Master״ — he called him wicked — and regarding what was sufficient [to answer], that he needed his wife: for it is an a fortiori argument — what is permitted to me was forbidden; what is forbidden, all the more so. If so, if he needed his female and wished, God forbid, to transgress the prohibition, behold his wife is before him, in whom there is only one prohibition, unlike going after the raven, in which there are two prohibitions." "Further, it may intend to say to him 'wicked' after the manner of what they of blessed memory said (Kiddushin 70a) — that whoever disqualifies others does so with his own blemish — and since he was wicked, he cast suspicion upon Noah. But as for the argument 'Had the prince of ... encountered me' etc., he gave him no reply, and therefore they of blessed memory said: an unanswerable argument he gave him etc.:" "<b>Further</b>, it is possible to say that by saying 'wicked' he hinted to him a reply to 'If the prince of ... should encounter me,' for he already knew of his wickedness. And even if the prince of ... etc. should encounter him, the world would not lack his kind, for the female argued against him — which is not so with the other birds that did not serve in the ark."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :register {:flags {4 ["God"]}}, :notes {5 "The abbreviation וכו׳ in both occurrences marks elided portions of the Talmudic anecdote (Kiddushin 22b–23a), reproduced here only in part."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:40:37.756584312Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}